  "problem": "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-3(x+1)^{2}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-3\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-3\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-1,0,1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/assets/03a5bf5134dc7d551b2602145c4c25081a2ad274f02c95845e5789b288e618a1.png)",
